        84.99369812011719,
        522.3762817382812,
        302.3926086425781
      ],
      "text": "clubs decided to set a limit of 90 minutes for each match. Before that it was a\nmore casual arrangement and this sometimes caused huge arguments and\nsometimes fights during matches when one team called the end of the game\nand the other team wanted to play on to try and score a winning goal. By 1878,\nthe number of teams in the Football League increased again. In addition,\nreferees started using whistles and electric lamps were installed on certain\npitches. This was a significant change, as games could then be played in the\nevenings all year round. In 1880, clubs began to charge fans for admission to\ngames, even though players were still amateurs and had other proper jobs.\nThat’s hard to imagine in the modern professional game, where top players\nearn significant sums of money from both playing and commercial activities.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        349.29205322265625,
        112.94929504394531,
        368.79345703125
      ],
      "text": "PART 3\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        383.1320495605469,
        105.86299896240234,
        402.6334533691406
      ],
      "text": "MAN:\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        75.12000274658203,
        403.23468017578125,
        423.50152587890625,
        422.0335998535156
      ],
      "text": "How are you getting on with the assignment on handwriting?\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        434.85205078125,
        128.062255859375,
        454.35345458984375
      ],
      "text": "WOMAN:\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        454.95367431640625,
        520.5321655273438,
        533.2736206054688
      ],
      "text": "Not too bad. You know, I hadn’t realised that children benefit in so many ways\nfrom learning to write. It’s such an important skill, and yet most people think\nhandwriting is less important than in the past, because people hardly ever\nwrite by hand these days.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        546.2110595703125,
        105.86299896240234,
        565.7124633789062
      ],
      "text": "MAN:\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        566.314697265625,
        486.8938903808594,
        604.9136352539062
      ],
      "text": "Yes, and all the evidence suggests children should learn to write by hand\nbefore they learn to type, not least because it helps their memory.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        617.85205078125,
        128.062255859375,
        637.3534545898438
      ],
      "text": "WOMAN:\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        637.834716796875,
        499.9777526855469,
        676.5526123046875
      ],
      "text": "That’s right. The physical act of writing helps children to remember letters.\nThat seems pretty obvious when you think about it.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        689.3710327148438,
        105.86299896240234,
        708.8724365234375
      ],
      "text": "MAN:\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        709.4747314453125,
        513.5640258789062,
        748.0736083984375
      ],
      "text": "What’s less obvious is how it helps develop their concentration. They have to\nsit still and focus on one thing.\n"
    }
